A bound menu item must unbind in its own dealloc, not leave it to the reaper.

23 MENU ITEMS PER RUN DIED COMPLAINING, and the complaint was accurate:

    An instance of class NSKVONotifying_NSMenuItem was deallocated while key value observers were
    still registered with it. Observation Info was leaked, and may even become mistakenly re-used
    by another object.

WHOSE OBSERVERS. Ours. With CIDER_TRACE_KVO=1 the registrations are 154 _NSKVOBinder observations
of objectValue on NSMenuItem, 18 of title, and 15 more: our own Cocoa bindings machinery, watching
the menu item as the SOURCE of a binding so that changes can be written back. Swift Publisher binds
its menu items to a controller, which the binding log shows as

    start observing binding between NSMenuItem.value alias objectValue and
    NSApplication.delegate.mainWCController.selection.nextPage

WHY THE EXISTING REAPER DOES NOT COVER IT. NSObject+BindingSupport attaches an associated object to
a bound source so that a class which never unbinds still gets its table entry reaped, and its own
comment names NSMenuItem as the reason it exists. That reaper does stop the dangling source crash.
But the runtime releases associated objects AFTER -dealloc has run, and KVO checks for surviving
observers DURING it, so at the moment of the check the binder was still registered. The reaper was
never too weak, only too late.

THE FIX IS THE ONE NSVIEW AND NSCONTROL ALREADY USE: call -_unbindAllBindings first thing in
-dealloc, which is early enough for the check to see a clean object. The reaper stays, because it
still covers every other class that can be bound and never unbinds.

MEASURED, same scenario, same instrument, one variable:
    warnings before   23
    warnings after     0
Both runs reach the same endpoint, exit=133 under zombies, with an identical 49603 byte starting
capture, so the scenario that produced the warnings still happened.

Task #250.
--- a/AppKit/NSMenu.subproj/NSMenuItem.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSMenu.subproj/NSMenuItem.m
@@ -167,6 +167,17 @@
 }
 
 - (void) dealloc {
+    /*
+     * UNBIND BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE, which NSView and NSControl already do and this class did not.
+     *
+     * The reaper in NSObject+BindingSupport exists because of that omission, and it does stop the
+     * dangling source crash, but it is an ASSOCIATED OBJECT: the runtime releases those AFTER
+     * -dealloc has run, and KVO checks for surviving observers DURING it. So the binder was still
+     * registered at the moment of the check and every bound menu item died complaining that its
+     * observation info had leaked, 23 of them in one Swift Publisher run. Unbinding here happens
+     * early enough for the check to see a clean object. Task #250.
+     */
+    [self _unbindAllBindings];
     [_title release];
     [_atitle release];
     [_keyEquivalent release];
